<h2>Disclosures</h2>
<small>Last updated November 10, 2026.</small>
<p>
  I strongly believe in clear disclosures by journalists and others writing
  about the topics they cover, and especially about cryptocurrencies, where
  undisclosed conflicts of interest are rampant. All outlets publishing
  cryptocurrency-related articles should have very clear and
  prominently-displayed disclosure policies.
</p>
<p>
  I write the <a href="https://www.citationneeded.news/" target="_blank">
    <i>Citation Needed</i>
  </a> newsletter,
  which is entirely reader-supported. I don&rsquo;t run ads on
  <i>Citation Needed</i>, <i>Web3 is Going Just Great</i>, or any of my other
  websites and projects. I also don&rsquo;t run ads on my social media accounts,
  with the exception of YouTube, which automatically shows ads on some of my videos
  and gives me a share of the revenue. I do not have a direct relationship with
  any advertisers, and don&rsquo;t choose the ads that run other than doing my best to
  configure YouTube not to run any ads related to cryptocurrencies or other topics I cover.
</p>
<p>
  I don&rsquo;t post sponsored content on <i>Citation Needed</i>, my various projects
  (<i>Web3 is Going Just Great</i>, <i>Tech Influence Watch</i>, etc.), this website, my
  social media accounts, or anywhere else. This will not change. No, I won&rsquo;t promote
  your project regardless of whether there is money changing hands, so please
  don&rsquo;t ask.
</p>
<p>
  Some generous people have sent money for <i>W3IGG</i>&rsquo;s cloud hosting and
  various other upkeep costs, which I otherwise pay out of pocket. There is more
  information on this, along with a brief expenses report, at the
  <a href="https://web3isgoinggreat.com/contribute" target="_blank">
    <i>W3IGG</i> – Contribute</a
    >
    page.
  </p>
  <p>
    I own small amounts (less than $500 worth in total at time of purchase) of crypto
    tokens or other crypto assets, which belong to an undisclosed address I use
    for research purposes. These do not represent a significant portion of my
    assets, and are used primarily to access token-gated projects and experiment
    with smart contracts rather than for any attempt to earn a return. If I do
    somehow end up with more money than I put in as a result, I intend to donate
    it.<sup>
      <a href="#footnote1">1</a>
    </sup>
  </p>
  <p>
    I hold no direct long or short positions pertaining to cryptocurrencies or
    crypto-related companies. I do have some investments in the stock market and
    other traditional forms of finance, which I pay someone much smarter about and
    more interested in finance than I am to handle. These may include broad index
    funds that hold small amounts of stock in companies in or adjacent to the crypto
    and AI industries; I don&rsquo;t direct those investments.
  </p>
  <p>
    I have no financial relationships with AI companies outside of paying for some consumer AI products
    as a customer; this doesn&rsquo;t come with any special access or arrangement.
  </p>
  <p>
    I&rsquo;ve been editing Wikipedia for about twenty years as
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:GorillaWarfare" target="_blank">GorillaWarfare</a>,
    and I&rsquo;ve been an administrator on the English Wikipedia for many years.
    I served on the English Wikipedia&rsquo;s Arbitration Committee from 2014 to
    2017 and from 2019 to 2020, and I currently serve on the Wikimedia
    Foundation&rsquo;s Product and Technology Advisory Council.
    All of these roles are volunteer and unpaid. I&rsquo;ve occasionally received
    incidental benefits, such as travel to conferences, but have otherwise
    received no financial benefit from Wikipedia or the Wikimedia Foundation.
  </p>
  <p>
    I don&rsquo;t accept money from candidates, campaigns, political parties,
    PACs, or other political organizations. I occasionally advise candidates
    and policymakers; this is always unpaid.
  </p>
  <p>
    From November 2022 to October 2023, I was a Fellow at Harvard&rsquo;s
    <a href="https://lil.law.harvard.edu/" target="_blank"
    >Library Innovation Lab</a
    >, which supported me in my work for a year. This fellowship was enabled by
    the
    <a
    href="https://lil.law.harvard.edu/about/democratizing-open-knowledge"
    target="_blank"
    >Democratizing Open Knowledge</a
    >
    program, which is funded by the
    <a href="https://ffdweb.org/" target="_blank"
    >Filecoin Foundation for the Decentralized Web</a
    >, a &ldquo;nonprofit organization committed to preserving humanity&rsquo;s most important
    information by fostering the development of open-source software and open
    protocols&rdquo;. As the name suggests, they are organized around the
    <a href="https://fil.org/about/" target="_blank">Filecoin</a> project, which is a
    cryptocurrency/blockchain project. The fellowship had no strings attached as
    far as my independence in researching and analyzing the crypto industry, and I
    was not obligated to do any work relating to Filecoin (nor was I obligated
    <i>not</i> to). My focus at the LIL did not involve Filecoin specifically.
  </p>
  <hr/>
  <p>
    While we&rsquo;re here, I&rsquo;ll mention that I don&rsquo;t speak on behalf of an employer or
    anyone else. I hope it goes without saying, but just to say it: nothing I say
    should be taken to be financial, investment, legal, professional, or any other
    kind of advice.
  </p>
  <p>
    <sup id="footnote1">[1]</sup>
    This has happened once. In 2024, I discovered 1.4&nbsp;SOL in an old research
    address had appreciated by $220, so I
    <a href="/micro/entry/202403301700">donated it to the EFF</a>.
  </p>
